1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:03,267
Right now I’m standing in front of the whiteboard at a neighborhood police box.

2
00:00:03,333 --> 00:00:08,167
In the corner, the officer is writing a tally mark. Already two strokes in.

3
00:00:08,233 --> 00:00:12,100
"Sir, we’re not the ones who send people to prison."

4
00:00:12,167 --> 00:00:14,333
Yes. That was the first stroke.

5
00:00:14,400 --> 00:00:16,367
Let’s rewind twenty minutes.

6
00:00:16,933 --> 00:00:24,333
We left the hospital with a bandaged wrist, and my kid’s bike was gone. The rack was completely empty.

7
00:00:24,900 --> 00:00:28,900
We crossed the street to the police box, and as I filled out the report, the next window got noisy.

8
00:00:28,967 --> 00:00:32,467
"He won’t pay back the money I lent him! Go arrest him!"

9
00:00:33,033 --> 00:00:35,367
My kid tugged at my sleeve.

10
00:00:35,433 --> 00:00:39,300
"Dad, if they catch a thief, does the thief go to jail? Right away?"

11
00:00:39,867 --> 00:00:44,667
"Of course. The police officer catches them and pops them right into jail."

12
00:00:44,733 --> 00:00:47,000
I said it firmly, for my kid’s sake.

13
00:00:47,067 --> 00:00:49,767
The officer looked up from the report.

14
00:00:49,833 --> 00:00:55,900
"Sir, our part is finding and investigating. The judge decides whether someone goes to prison."

15
00:00:55,967 --> 00:00:57,933
In the corner of the whiteboard, one stroke.

16
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:02,100
Then a caption on the wall TV: “Detention warrant rejected.”

17
00:01:02,167 --> 00:01:04,767
"Rejected. I guess they’re not guilty."

18
00:01:05,333 --> 00:01:12,800
"Ma’am, “rejected” doesn’t mean innocent. It means there’s no need to hold them while investigating. The investigation and trial go on as usual."

19
00:01:12,867 --> 00:01:13,700
Two strokes.

20
00:01:14,267 --> 00:01:19,133
The caption changed: “Prosecutors’ Office closes after 78 years.”

21
00:01:19,200 --> 00:01:23,200
"If the prosecutors disappear, who sends the bad guys to trial now?"

22
00:01:23,267 --> 00:01:26,333
My wife and I turned to look at the officer at the same time.

23
00:01:26,900 --> 00:01:32,500
While the officer looked for the CCTV, I hid behind the standee by the water dispenser and turned on my phone.

24
00:01:32,567 --> 00:01:36,533
The creature known as Dad, when cornered, will hide even inside a police station.

25
00:01:36,600 --> 00:01:40,833
To be fair, it’s confusing. Detention, rejection, indictment, sentencing request, ruling.

26
00:01:40,900 --> 00:01:45,167
But before all that, there were two bigger words. Criminal. And civil.

27
00:01:45,233 --> 00:01:49,700
Today we look at just two things: what kind of case it is, and who decides.

28
00:01:50,267 --> 00:01:52,033
"Sir, come over here a moment."

29
00:01:52,600 --> 00:01:58,200
The officer drew a fork in the road on the whiteboard. Left is criminal, right is civil.

30
00:01:58,267 --> 00:02:04,167
Criminal cases are where the state asks for punishment: theft, assault, taking money by deceit.

31
00:02:04,233 --> 00:02:08,167
The victim reports it, but it’s the state that steps up and seeks the punishment.

32
00:02:08,733 --> 00:02:13,900
Civil cases are where people sort out who owes what: money, belongings, promises.

33
00:02:13,967 --> 00:02:20,067
Not punishment, but a ruling on who gives how much to whom. No police or prosecutors involved.

34
00:02:20,633 --> 00:02:29,267
As for the man at the next window: if the borrower never meant to repay and tricked him, that’s fraud, a criminal case. If they simply can’t pay because times are hard, that’s civil.

35
00:02:29,333 --> 00:02:34,700
And punishment doesn’t automatically bring the money back, so getting it back needs a separate civil case anyway.

36
00:02:34,767 --> 00:02:39,367
My kid’s bike, if someone took it, is criminal. Let’s follow that road.

37
00:02:39,933 --> 00:02:44,700
First square: investigation. The police handle it. The police are the ones who find.

38
00:02:44,767 --> 00:02:50,033
The person they find isn’t a “criminal” but a “suspect,” because nothing has been decided yet.

39
00:02:50,600 --> 00:02:55,167
Next square: the warrant. Holding someone for long needs a judge’s permission.

40
00:02:55,233 --> 00:03:02,600
The police apply to a prosecutor, the prosecutor requests it from a judge, and the judge calls the person in and decides face to face.

41
00:03:03,167 --> 00:03:11,300
OK to hold: issued. No need to hold: rejected. They’re held only if they might flee or destroy evidence.

42
00:03:11,367 --> 00:03:14,867
The law says it too: investigations are, as a rule, done without detention.

43
00:03:15,433 --> 00:03:21,800
Next square: indictment. This is the prosecutor’s seat. They decide whether to send this person to trial.

44
00:03:21,867 --> 00:03:25,900
Once sent, the name changes again: from suspect to “defendant.”

45
00:03:26,467 --> 00:03:33,533
Last square: the trial. When the prosecutor says “please sentence them to so many years,” that’s the sentencing request — a request.

46
00:03:33,600 --> 00:03:36,600
The one who decides is the judge. The ruling is the decision.

47
00:03:37,167 --> 00:03:42,167
Until that ruling is final, the person is presumed innocent. The Constitution says so.

48
00:03:42,733 --> 00:03:51,733
The right-hand road, civil, is much shorter. You file a complaint with the court; the filer is the plaintiff and the other side the defendant — a similar word to the criminal “defendant,” but a different one.

49
00:03:51,800 --> 00:03:57,200
The judge hears both sides and rules. No warrants, no detention, no rejections.

50
00:03:57,767 --> 00:04:02,700
Now, my kid’s question: with the prosecutors gone, who sends people to trial?

51
00:04:02,767 --> 00:04:06,967
On the 2nd of this month, the sign of the 78-year-old Prosecutors’ Office came down.

52
00:04:07,533 --> 00:04:11,633
The police handle investigations; truly big cases go to the new Serious Crimes Investigation Agency.

53
00:04:11,700 --> 00:04:18,300
Prosecutors now belong to the Public Prosecution Office and only send cases to trial and apply for warrants. They can’t investigate directly.

54
00:04:18,367 --> 00:04:20,933
Judges stay the same, and so do civil cases.

55
00:04:21,500 --> 00:04:30,567
Opinions were sharply divided. One side says power must be split so it doesn’t pile up in one place; the other worries a sudden split could leave gaps. We’ll have to watch and see.

56
00:04:31,133 --> 00:04:32,433
So, to sum up:

57
00:04:32,500 --> 00:04:34,800
Criminal is the road where the state asks for punishment.

58
00:04:34,867 --> 00:04:37,433
Civil is the road where people sort out their shares.

59
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:40,467
On either road, the one who decides in the end is the judge.

60
00:04:41,900 --> 00:04:46,533
I sat my kid down after the report was done and explained it again in front of the game board.

61
00:04:46,600 --> 00:04:51,633
"Earlier, Dad said the police officer puts people in jail, right? That was wrong."

62
00:04:51,700 --> 00:04:56,167
"The police are the ones who find. They find out who took it and what happened."

63
00:04:56,233 --> 00:05:00,900
"The prosecutor is the one who takes the person to the judge and says, ‘Your Honor, please look at this person’s case.’"

64
00:05:01,467 --> 00:05:07,433
"The judge hears both sides and decides whether it really happened, and if so, what to do."

65
00:05:07,500 --> 00:05:15,333
"If a friend doesn’t give back a toy you lent, that’s two people sorting out what’s whose, so the judge says ‘let’s do it this way.’ That’s civil."

66
00:05:15,400 --> 00:05:22,200
"If one person finds, takes, and decides everything, nobody can fix a mistake. That’s why it’s split up on purpose."

67
00:05:22,267 --> 00:05:26,467
"And until the judge decides, nobody is allowed to call that person a thief."

68
00:05:27,033 --> 00:05:28,733
"Even the person who took my bike?"

69
00:05:28,800 --> 00:05:30,267
"Yes. Even that person."

70
00:05:30,333 --> 00:05:35,700
My kid looked at the report again and erased one box with the eraser on the pencil.

71
00:05:35,767 --> 00:05:40,833
In the spot where they’d written “thief,” they slowly wrote again: “the one who took it.”

72
00:05:41,400 --> 00:05:49,367
If the police catch you, you go to jail. If it’s rejected, you’re innocent. If you don’t pay back money, the police take you away. It wasn’t just us who thought so, right?

73
00:05:49,433 --> 00:05:53,067
Then the officer turned the monitor toward us.

74
00:05:53,133 --> 00:05:56,600
"Sir, the bike… it’s still there, next to a park bench."

75
00:05:57,167 --> 00:06:02,400
The park where my kid fell that morning. I’d run to the hospital carrying them, and I’d left the bike behind.

76
00:06:02,467 --> 00:06:04,767
In the corner of the whiteboard, the third stroke.

77
00:06:04,833 --> 00:06:07,133
"The one who took it was Dad."

78
00:06:07,200 --> 00:06:09,600
"…Let’s stop by the park, and then go get ice cream."

79
00:06:10,167 --> 00:06:14,200
The man from the next window left with a blank civil complaint form in hand.

80
00:06:14,767 --> 00:06:16,567
The next episode is about this question.
